Building commissioning differs from conventional testing in several key ways. Conventional testing typically focuses on individual equipment, components, or subsystems to verify their proper functioning and whether they meet design performance parameters. In contrast, building commissioning has three key distinctions:

Scope Difference: Commissioning covers the entire system or project, involving a broader range of aspects;

Documentation Requirements: Commissioning not only completes the testing work but also requires establishing a comprehensive documentation system, with records starting from the design phase;

Quality Control: Commissioning includes Quality Assurance and Quality Control (QA/QC) functions. The commissioning agent must participate in the project during the design phase, reviewing design documents from the consultant team, including technical specifications and drawings.
